:answer/value "\\frac{2}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d472f68-fdb3-5afd-b2e8-f314fcc223d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346566} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c46e3b-c984-5aea-8176-46b44457b35d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62470d93-171f-5467-9d9d-53ce99023b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346569} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa58980-a71c-5f19-b699-b9b4401a6f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346570} {:answer/id #uuid "26a3dd49-563a-58a5-aae8-d5925dab0a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346571} {:answer/id #uuid "561ae5b3-5ba7-55da-af16-e0e846f3f21b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346572} {:answer/id #uuid "825c5252-f637-591f-8f4e-d90e00cfe44b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a3dd49-563a-58a5-aae8-d5925dab0a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346571} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2a9f8f9-45de-58d9-90fb-ef18439e87f6" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16f637ea-4c45-59a4-9cae-b0d8a871eae2" :question/math-academy-id "q-340732" :question/problem "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/2}.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-2}}\nthe integral is divergent.\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-3}}\nthe integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating our integral in the convergent case, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{{e}^{t/2}}=\\frac{2}{2s-1},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{t/2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ or $s=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{1/2-s}{e}^{(1/2-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1/2-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}-\\frac{1}{s-1/2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{1}{2}) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-1/2)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>\\frac{1}{2}$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}-\\frac{1}{s-1/2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}-\\frac{1}{s-1/2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/2}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{2}{2s-1},$ defined for $s>\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2fd0466-9f47-5adc-911a-f297858951b1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Real Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1e7fd21-313d-5d1e-831e-90ed46f21a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346576} {:answer/id #uuid "787c6e10-a6dc-552f-80e7-0a588c48cd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346577} {:answer/id #uuid "9976918f-1eab-5080-ae25-b4d0651b84b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346578} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ef4697-02d6-583f-8648-26853c0b72a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "